  "worked_solution": "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\nf(x+4\\pi )=f((x+2\\pi )+2\\pi )=f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=2\\pi .$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2\\pi ) & =f(0+2\\pi )=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nF(2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
